program            = 005_attribute_packed_aligned
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = GCC __attribute__((packed)) and __attribute__((aligned)) and their observable effect on aggregate size, alignment and member offsets
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. There is no signed overflow: the largest magnitude used is
-2000000000, well inside the int range, and it is never negated, doubled or otherwise
grown. No shift operator is used. There is no aliasing violation, because every member is
read and written only through its own declared type. Every member of every object is
assigned before it is read, and the two objects with static storage duration are
zero-initialized before their explicit assignments in any case. No one-past-end pointer is
formed. No object is modified twice between sequence points. Every printf call receives at
most one side-effecting argument.

NO PADDING BYTE IS EVER READ. Reading padding is unspecified, so the program inspects only
members it has written and never examines the bytes between them. It also never depends on
the addresses of unrelated objects.

MEMBER OFFSETS COME EXCLUSIVELY FROM __builtin_offsetof, which is a compile-time constant
and takes no address at run time. Measured with gcc 13.3.0: taking the address of a packed
member produces "error: taking address of packed member of 'struct p' may result in an
unaligned pointer value [-Werror=address-of-packed-member]", because
-Waddress-of-packed-member is on by default and -Werror makes it fatal. The program
therefore never forms a pointer to a packed member and never computes an offset by
subtracting member addresses. __builtin_offsetof is also required because this program
includes no header: the corpus's only sanctioned inclusion is area 07's <stdarg.h>, so
offsetof from stddef.h is out of reach even though stddef.h is itself in bcc's bundled
freestanding set.

Every character-sized member is explicitly signed char, never plain char, and the
round-trip prints it as int. char was measured as signed on x86_64 and i686 but unsigned
on aarch64 and riscv64, so the negative value -7 would print as 249 on a target where
plain char is unsigned. The explicit signedness avoids that trap.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0.

Nothing target-restricting is observed, so all four targets are compared. The printed
layout values are identical on x86_64, i686, aarch64 and riscv64 because on all four int
is 4 bytes with 4-byte alignment, short is 2 with 2, and signed char is 1 with 1:

  plain_s   { signed char; int; short }                     a@0, 3 bytes padding, b@4,
            c@8, natural size 10 rounded up to the 4-byte alignment => size 12, align 4
  packed_s  packed                                          no padding at all => a@0, b@1,
            c@5, size 7, align 1. Packing is alignment-independent BY CONSTRUCTION, so
            this layout is identical on every target.
  aligned_s { signed char; int } aligned(16)                a@0, 3 bytes padding, b@4,
            natural size 8 rounded up to 16 => size 16, align 16
  both_s    packed + aligned(8)                             a@0, b@1, c@5, packed size 7
            rounded up to 8 => size 8, align 8
  attr_packed_saves = 12 - 7 = 5

No address is ever printed. The alignment claim is expressed as a RESIDUE, addr % 16 ==
0, printed as the boolean 1, because an address differs per build and per target and
would destroy byte-exact comparison. A pointer fact is printed only as a difference, a
comparison or an alignment residue, never as an address, because only those three are
reproducible across builds, targets and optimization levels.

The pointer cast is unsigned long, NOT unsigned long long. unsigned long is exactly
pointer-width on all four targets: 4 bytes under ILP32 on i686 and 8 bytes under LP64 on
x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64. This removes any possibility of a pointer-size cast
diagnostic on i686, where casting a pointer to a wider integer is diagnosed as
-Wpointer-to-int-cast and escalated to an error by this program's gate.

Every sizeof and _Alignof result is cast to unsigned before being passed to %u, because
size_t width differs across targets and an uncast size_t would be a genuine format
mismatch on LP64 as well as a -Wformat error under -Werror. All the values are small, so
no cast truncates anything.

Both packed and aligned are documented as supported: the supported attribute set at
docs/technical-specifications.md line 506 is packed, aligned, section, unused, deprecated,
visibility, format. This program therefore tests documented functionality and carries NO
expected-divergence marker.

Output is identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
attr_plain_size=12
attr_plain_align=4
attr_plain_off=0 4 8
attr_packed_size=7
attr_packed_align=1
attr_packed_off=0 1 5
attr_aligned_size=16
attr_aligned_align=16
attr_aligned_off=0 4
attr_both_size=8
attr_both_align=8
attr_both_off=0 1 5
attr_packed_saves=5
attr_packed_roundtrip=65 123456789 -321
attr_plain_roundtrip=-7 -2000000000 32767
attr_static_packed=1 2 3
attr_var_align16=1
attr_var_value=99
END
